	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Proven components vs a clean rewrite</h3>
		<p>
			This is the real decision. Mailcow doesn't implement mail protocols itself: it
			packages Postfix, Dovecot, Rspamd, SOGo, ClamAV and MariaDB, components with decades
			of production hardening between them, into one managed Docker stack. Every strange
			edge case in SMTP and IMAP has long since been hit and fixed upstream.
		</p>
		<p>
			Stalwart takes the opposite approach: SMTP, IMAP, POP3, JMAP, CalDAV, CardDAV and
			WebDAV, all reimplemented from scratch in memory-safe Rust and shipped as a single
			binary, with a published security audit. That buys a coherent design and very modest
			resource use. The cost is a younger codebase that is still pre-1.0: its own roadmap
			says the next milestone is finalising the database schema before version 1.0.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Hardware</h3>
		<p>
			The gap is large. Stalwart idles at around 100 MB and documents 1 GB of RAM as
			comfortable for 5 to 10 users. Mailcow's documented minimum is 6 GiB of RAM plus
			1 GiB of swap, with 8 GiB recommended for the same 5 to 10 users, because it runs
			SOGo, ClamAV, MariaDB and the rest of its stack. Stalwart also runs natively on Linux,
			macOS, FreeBSD and Windows; Mailcow needs Linux with Docker on full virtualisation.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Protocols and clients</h3>
		<p>
			Stalwart is the more protocol-complete server: JMAP for mail, calendars and contacts,
			plus CalDAV, CardDAV and WebDAV file storage, all built in. Mailcow covers SMTP, IMAP
			and POP3 through Postfix and Dovecot, and gets calendar, contacts and Exchange
			ActiveSync from SOGo.
		</p>
		<p>
			The practical difference shows up in the browser and on phones. Mailcow's SOGo is a
			full webmail and groupware web app, and ActiveSync means phones sync mail, calendar
			and contacts natively. Stalwart's web interface is for administration and account
			self-service, not reading mail, and it doesn't speak ActiveSync, so users rely on
			IMAP, JMAP or DAV clients, or a webmail you add yourself.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Filtering</h3>
		<p>
			Mailcow ships Rspamd for spam and ClamAV for viruses, both preconfigured. Stalwart has
			its own built-in spam filter with a statistical classifier, Pyzor and spam traps (LLM
			analysis is an Enterprise feature); antivirus means running ClamAV yourself and
			connecting it as a milter.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Scale and storage</h3>
		<p>
			If you need more than one machine, this is Stalwart's strongest ground. It clusters
			natively, coordinates through peer-to-peer or Kafka, NATS or Redis, runs on Kubernetes
			or Docker Swarm, and stores data in your choice of RocksDB, PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite,
			FoundationDB, S3-compatible or Azure storage; read replicas and sharded storage are
			Enterprise features. Mailcow is a single-host stack; high
			availability means building replication and failover around it yourself.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Upgrades</h3>
		<p>
			Neither has an automated update safety net. Mailcow upgrades with
			<code>update.sh</code>, which pulls new images and restarts containers. Stalwart's
			documentation says plainly that there is no automated upgrade tool yet: patch releases
			are safe, but minor releases may require configuration changes or data migrations, so
			it recommends reading the release notes first and upgrading only when necessary.
			Neither takes a pre-update snapshot, runs a health-check gate or rolls back on failure.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Licensing and cost</h3>
		<p>
			Mailcow is GPL-3.0 with no paid edition; its maintainers offer ServerCow support
			contracts. Stalwart is dual-licensed: the AGPL-3.0 core is free, and a proprietary
			Enterprise edition, priced per mailbox, adds multi-tenancy, SCIM provisioning, LLM spam
			filtering, mail undelete, read replicas and sharded storage, live telemetry and priority
			support.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Programmatic sending</h3>
		<p>
			Neither is a transactional sending platform. Stalwart's JMAP can submit mail and its
			webhooks report events, but your application integrates against a mail protocol.
			Mailcow's API is administrative, and applications send through SMTP submission as a
			real mailbox. There are no domain-scoped sending keys, per-domain rate limits or
			delivery analytics in either.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Who each is best for</h2>
		<div class="feature-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<div class="feature-card">
				<h3>Choose Stalwart if</h3>
				<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
					<li>You want JMAP, or CalDAV/CardDAV/WebDAV built into the server itself.</li>
					<li>You need clustering, high availability or a specific storage backend.</li>
					<li>RAM is tight, or you want to run outside Docker or Linux.</li>
					<li>You're comfortable with pre-1.0 software and reading release notes before each upgrade.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="feature-card">
				<h3>Choose Mailcow if</h3>
				<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
					<li>You want the proven Postfix and Dovecot core rather than a rewrite.</li>
					<li>Your users need webmail and phone sync (ActiveSync) on day one.</li>
					<li>You want Rspamd and ClamAV preconfigured.</li>
					<li>You have 6–8 GiB of RAM for a single, self-contained host.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>The third option: Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>
			If what's missing from <em>both</em> is a transactional sending API, a proven mail
			core that doesn't need 6 GiB of RAM, or an update you don't have to supervise, that's
			the gap Vectis Mail is built to close. It runs the same proven Postfix and Dovecot
			core as Mailcow, with Rspamd by default, installed by one guided command:
		</p>
		<div class="feature-card" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
				<li>Runs on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS from 2 GB of RAM without ClamAV (4 GB recommended), in 10 containers by default.</li>
				<li>Domains, mailboxes, aliases and API keys managed from an admin dashboard or the REST API, taking effect immediately with no restarts.</li>
				<li>A 40+ endpoint REST API: transactional sending (single and batch, with attachments), HMAC-signed inbound webhooks, domain-scoped API keys with per-domain rate limits, and analytics.</li>
				<li>Atomic 6-phase updates (snapshot, migrate, pull, deploy, health-check, complete) with automatic rollback, and <code>vectis verify</code> to prove the box runs exactly what was published.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
		<p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
			To be equally clear about where Vectis Mail doesn't fit: it's Docker-only and
			single-node, and it doesn't ship JMAP, calendar, contacts or ActiveSync today. If
			groupware or a multi-node cluster is the core requirement, it isn't your answer. See
			the dedicated <a href="/alternatives/stalwart/">Vectis Mail vs Stalwart</a> and
			<a href="/alternatives/mailcow/">Vectis Mail vs Mailcow</a> comparisons for the full
			detail on each.
		</p>
		<div class="hero-buttons" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<a href="/getting-started/installation/" class="btn-primary">Install Vectis Mail free</a>
			<a href="/pricing/" class="btn-secondary">View pricing</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Stalwart or Mailcow better?</h3>
				<p>
					They make opposite bets. Mailcow assembles long-proven components (Postfix,
					Dovecot, Rspamd, SOGo, ClamAV) into an 18-service Docker stack with webmail,
					groupware and phone sync included. Stalwart reimplements every protocol in one
					Rust binary, adding JMAP, clustering and pluggable storage on a fraction of the
					RAM, but it is still pre-1.0 and has no bundled webmail. Choose on whether you
					value proven internals and a complete out-of-the-box suite (Mailcow) or a modern
					single binary that scales out (Stalwart).
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Stalwart production-ready?</h3>
				<p>
					Stalwart describes itself as feature complete, but it is still pre-1.0 (v0.16.25 as of October 2026), and its own
					upgrade documentation says there is no automated upgrade tool yet: minor
					releases may require configuration changes or data migrations, so it recommends
					reading the release notes and upgrading only when necessary. Version 1.0 is
					planned to finalise the database schema.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>How much RAM do Stalwart and Mailcow need?</h3>
				<p>
					Stalwart idles at roughly 100 MB, and its documentation says 1 GB of RAM is
					generally sufficient for 5 to 10 users. Mailcow's documented minimum is 6 GiB of
					RAM plus 1 GiB of swap with the default configuration, with 8 GiB recommended
					for about 5 to 10 users, because it runs SOGo, ClamAV, MariaDB and the rest of
					its 18-service stack.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Stalwart have webmail like Mailcow?</h3>
				<p>
					No. Stalwart's web interface is for administration and account self-service; it
					doesn't bundle a webmail client, so you add one yourself or rely on desktop and
					mobile mail apps. Mailcow bundles SOGo, which provides webmail, calendar,
					contacts and Exchange ActiveSync in one web app.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does either have a transactional sending API?</h3>
				<p>
					Not in the SendGrid sense. Stalwart offers JMAP, which can submit mail, plus
					event webhooks, so an application has to integrate against a mail protocol.
					Mailcow's API is administrative, and applications send through SMTP submission as
					a real mailbox. Neither offers domain-scoped sending keys, per-domain rate limits
					or delivery analytics. Vectis Mail is a self-hosted platform built to add that
					sending API alongside mailbox hosting.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

